Operations
==========

Requirements
------------

Node 20 or newer. The backend must be running and reachable; this repository
has no mock layer and no fixtures.


Environment
-----------

Create .env in the repository root.

	VITE_API_PATH=http://localhost:8000/api
	VITE_LOGIN_VIDEO_URL=            optional background video on the login page

Only variables prefixed VITE_ are exposed to client code, and everything in
this file ships to the browser. Never put a secret here.

VITE_API_PATH must end in /api. The socket origin is derived from it by
stripping that suffix.

The backend's CLIENT_ORIGINS must include this app's origin or every request
fails CORS before it reaches a route.


Scripts
-------

	npm run dev       Vite dev server, http://localhost:5173
	npm run build     tsc -b then vite build, output in dist/
	npm run preview   serve the built output
	npm run lint      eslint

Run npm run build before any handover. The dev server does not typecheck;
tsc -b does, and it is the only thing that will catch a type drift between a
Types file and what the backend actually sends.


First run against a fresh backend
---------------------------------

1. Start Postgres and the backend. The backend seeds a bootstrap admin from
   its SEED_ADMIN_* variables.
2. Sign in here with those credentials.
3. Create a challenge. Publishing notifies every student, by socket and push,
   so use a disposable challenge for testing.
4. Log a result from the mobile app; it appears in the validation queue here.


Assets
------

public/avatars/faculty1..faculty9/1..5.png and public/facultyBadges/1..9.png
are copies of the mobile app's art. Vite serves public/ at the web root, so
they are referenced as absolute paths. If the mobile art changes, copy it here
in the same commit; nothing keeps the two in sync automatically.


Deployment
----------

The build is static. Any static host works, with two requirements:

* All routes must fall back to index.html, or a refresh on /app/quests will
  404. Client-side routing owns every path.
* VITE_API_PATH is baked in at build time, not read at runtime. A different
  API endpoint means a different build.

Before real use: serve over HTTPS. Access tokens are held in localStorage and
sent as bearer credentials.